**generator.ts** has a null-dereference bug that would throw when `experimentalReactComponentMeta` is enabled but no matching component is found. `findMatchingComponent` can return `undefined`, and the existing early-return guard only fires when `!experimentalReactComponentMeta && !hasDocgen` — so if `experimentalReactComponentMeta=true` and the component lookup misses, execution falls through to bare `component.reactDocgen` accesses.

The `debug` field is appended to the manifest meta object at runtime but never declared in `ComponentsManifest.meta`. That's a type/API contract mismatch.

The `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars: 'off'` override in `.eslintrc.js` sits inside what appears to be a broad React-file override. Turning off the entire rule there seems heavier than needed since the actual intent (silencing `_props` in test fixtures) could be handled by the `_` prefix convention the code already uses.

On `ComponentMetaManager.ts`: `fs.watch` with `{ recursive: true }` was only added for Linux via `inotify` in Node 20; on older runtimes it throws, and the silent `logger.debug` catch means file watching simply stops working without any warning to the user.

`extractDefaultsFromSourceFile` parses source files on every call with no caching. For monorepos with many package-import components it will repeatedly read and parse the same files.

The deleted test cases in `reactDocgenTypescript.test.ts` documented RDT's known failures — good motivation for the new engine. Keeping them as `it.skip(...)` with a comment would preserve that intent as a regression guard.

